window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"what-is-ldndrc":"<p>A robotics workshop needs a room full of gaming laptops and one machine that can show everyone a robot. That normally means a cloud account, a GPU server, and a network engineer. LDNDRC replaces all three with the laptops people already brought.</p>\n<p>The product is a <strong>local-first ROS 2 lab</strong>. High-end laptops become compute. Everything else becomes a thin client. The only thing a student installs is nothing — they open a browser.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-clocks\">The three clocks</h2><p>Everything in the system runs on one of three clocks, and keeping them separate is most of the architecture:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clock</th>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Cluster</td>\n<td>minutes</td>\n<td>Nodes join, images import, the control panel rolls out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Session</td>\n<td>seconds</td>\n<td>A student asks for a workspace and gets one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Robot</td>\n<td>milliseconds</td>\n<td>Gazebo physics and the websocket scene stream</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A student never sees the first clock. They see a launch screen, and then they see their robot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-student-actually-gets\">What a student actually gets</h2><p>One login, four hostnames, one private pod:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>control.ros-platform.local</code> — the panel: login, launch, stop</li>\n<li><code>editor.ros-platform.local</code> — code-server, VS Code with terminals</li>\n<li><code>desktop.ros-platform.local</code> — a full Linux desktop over WebRTC</li>\n<li><code>gazebo.ros-platform.local</code> — the 3D scene, streamed</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cluster, the pods, and the images are invisible. That is the point: the interface is the product.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-is-not\">What it is not</h2><p>It is not a general Kubernetes tutorial, and it is not a robot simulator. It is the plumbing that turns laptops into a classroom, built so that a single instructor can run it without a cloud bill.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/healthz\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># {&quot;status&quot;:&quot;ok&quot;}</span></code></pre></div><p>If that answers, a control panel is alive somewhere and you can start asking it questions. Next: <a href=\"#/roles\" class=\"topic-link\">Hosts, Guests, Operators</a> to see which machine you are standing at.</p>","roles":"<p>There are three roles, and the interesting part is that they are assigned by <strong>hardware</strong>, not by a permission table. A laptop does not choose to be a Host. It either clears the bar or it does not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"host\">Host</h2><p>A gaming or workstation laptop: at least 8 CPU cores, at least 16 GB of RAM, and an NVIDIA GPU. It runs <code>./join-cluster.sh</code>, installs the K3s agent, and gets labelled <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/role=host</code>. From then on it is a scheduling target for student pods. It should stay plugged in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"guest\">Guest</h2><p>Any laptop with Chrome or Firefox and WebGL. It installs nothing, joins no cluster, and holds no credentials beyond a browser cookie. It consumes a session that is scheduled on somebody else&#39;s Host.</p>\n<h2 id=\"operator\">Operator</h2><p>The master laptop, with a static LAN IP and sleep disabled. It runs the K3s server, the control panel, the ingress, and the approval console. There is exactly one, and it is the only machine a student never thinks about.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-split-works\">Why this split works</h2><p>The three roles map cleanly onto three threat and failure models:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>Guest</strong> can be compromised without touching anyone else&#39;s data — every session is its own pod with its own home volume.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Host</strong> only ever contributes capacity. It runs other people&#39;s pods but has no panel credentials.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Operator</strong> holds the JWT secret and the K3s token. Losing a Host is an inconvenience; losing the Operator is a rebuild.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is also why onboarding is operator-gated: a laptop that clears the hardware bar still cannot become a Host until a human approves it. See <a href=\"#/nodes-api\" class=\"topic-link\">Register, approve, poll</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-the-boundary-actually-sits\">Where the boundary actually sits</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">Guest browser ──cookie──▶ control panel ──JWT──▶ session pod on a Host\n                                │\n                                └─ K3s token ─▶ Host agent (approved once)</code></pre></div><p>A Guest never talks to a Host directly. All traffic is HTTP, WebSocket, or WebRTC through the panel&#39;s gateway — which is the next idea, <a href=\"#/dds-isolation\" class=\"topic-link\">Why DDS never leaves the pod</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl get nodes -L node-role.kubernetes.io/role</code></pre></div><p>The <code>ROLE</code> column is the whole role model in one line.</p>","lan-first":"<p>The requirement that shaped everything else: the platform must work with <strong>no cloud, no dedicated server, and no internet dependency</strong> during a workshop.</p>\n<p>That sounds like a deployment detail. It is really a design constraint that eliminates whole categories of solution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-rules-out\">What it rules out</h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Managed Kubernetes.</strong> No EKS, no GKE, no control plane you do not own.</li>\n<li><strong>External identity.</strong> No OAuth provider. Login has to work when the uplink is down.</li>\n<li><strong>Hosted object storage.</strong> Student home directories live on a PVC in the cluster.</li>\n<li><strong>CDNs.</strong> Fonts, viewer assets, and scripts ship with the image.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-forces\">What it forces</h2><p><strong>A static address.</strong> The master needs a predictable LAN IP, because four hostnames have to resolve to it. There is no DNS server, so Guests get <code>/etc/hosts</code> entries or mDNS.</p>\n<p><strong>A hardware tier.</strong> Since compute is donated, the platform must decide which laptops qualify — that is <a href=\"#/hardware-gate\" class=\"topic-link\">The hardware gate</a>, and it exists because of this constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>An enrollment path that works before the network works.</strong> A laptop that has never seen the cluster cannot download an agent from it. The answer is a captive portal served on a separate onboarding access point: <a href=\"#/captive-portal\" class=\"topic-link\">The captive onboarding portal</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Local images.</strong> A 10 GB workspace image cannot be pulled from a registry over a workshop uplink. It is built, tagged immutably, exported as a tarball, and imported node by node.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-trade-off\">The trade-off</h2><p>You get predictability, zero recurring cost, and data that never leaves the room. You pay in operational sharp edges: IP addresses are manual, image distribution is manual, and the master is a single point of failure with no failover.</p>\n<p>For a classroom that is the right trade. For a product you would sell, it is not — which is why the roadmap keeps <a href=\"#/roadmap\" class=\"topic-link\">What is still left</a> honest about what is still missing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">ip -4 addr show | grep inet</code></pre></div><p>If the master&#39;s address is DHCP-assigned, this constraint is already being violated. Pin it.</p>","dds-isolation":"<p>ROS 2 uses DDS for discovery and message passing. DDS is chatty: it multicasts, it discovers peers automatically, and it assumes a flat network. Put twenty student robots on one LAN and every node sees every other node.</p>\n<p>The platform&#39;s answer is not a firewall rule. It is a <strong>network namespace</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-mechanism\">The mechanism</h2><p>A Kubernetes pod shares one network namespace across all its containers. <code>code-server</code>, Selkies, Gazebo, and the ROS processes all see the same <code>localhost</code>. DDS multicast never crosses the pod boundary because, as far as the kernel is concerned, there is nowhere to cross to.</p>\n<p>Flannel — the CNI — carries only HTTP, WebSocket, and WebRTC between pods. No DDS packet ever traverses the overlay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"defense-in-depth\">Defense in depth</h2><p>Namespace isolation alone is enough in theory. The platform adds two more layers anyway:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <strong>unique <code>ROS_DOMAIN_ID</code> per session pod</strong>. Two pods that somehow shared a network would still not discover each other&#39;s topics.</li>\n<li><code>ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE=LOCALHOST</code>, which pins discovery to loopback even inside the pod.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The second layer costs nothing and turns a subtle misconfiguration into a non-event.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-not-just-use-separate-vlans\">Why not just use separate VLANs?</h2><p>VLANs require switch configuration and physical control of the network. The workshop happens on whatever Wi-Fi the venue has. A pod namespace is a property of the deployment, not of the venue.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-buys\">What this buys</h2><ul>\n<li>A student cannot accidentally publish <code>/cmd_vel</code> into another student&#39;s robot.</li>\n<li>Two students can run the same world and the same topic names with no coordination.</li>\n<li>Teardown is <code>DELETE /api/sessions/{id}</code> — the namespace goes with it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The cost is that cross-session collaboration is impossible by construction. If two students need to share a topic, they have to share a session. That is a deliberate limitation, not a missing feature.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> &lt;session-pod&gt; -- <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">env</span> | grep ROS_DOMAIN_ID\nkubectl -n ldndrc <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec</span> &lt;session-pod&gt; -- <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">env</span> | grep ROS_AUTOMATIC_DISCOVERY_RANGE</code></pre></div><p>Compare the domain IDs of two sessions. They must differ. Then read <a href=\"#/ros-domain-id\" class=\"topic-link\">Unique ROS_DOMAIN_ID per pod</a> for how the value is assigned.</p>"});
